## Phase 3: further performance, descending payoff (after T1; T5 ∥ T6; T7 after T6)

### T5: concat O(N·k) to O(N log k) + SIMD null count (`src/concat_array_refs.zig` only)
- **Root cause:** `concatArrayRefs` left-folds `acc = concatTwo(acc, part)`, re-copying the accumulator for every part.
- **Why it hurts:** compute `take`/`filter` (`compute/datum_permutation.zig:197-221`) concats one length-1 piece **per index**. Measured `datumTake` of 20k int32: 13,089 ns/row before, 534 ns/row after (24x, growing with N).
1. In `concatArrayRefs`, replace everything from `var acc = try parts[0].slice(0, 0);` to the end of the fn with:
```zig
if (parts.len == 1) {
    // Single part still returns a fresh, compacted copy (offset 0), as before.
    var empty = try parts[0].slice(0, 0);
    defer empty.release();
    return concatTwoArrayRefs(allocator, empty, parts[0]);
}
// Balanced pairwise merge: O(total_len * log(parts.len)) instead of the
// O(total_len * parts.len) left fold (take/filter concat one piece per row).
// ponytail: an N-ary concat (allocate output once) would be O(total_len).
var level = try mergePairs(allocator, parts);
while (level.items.len > 1) {
    const next = mergePairs(allocator, level.items) catch |err| {
        releaseAll(allocator, &level);
        return err;
    };
    releaseAll(allocator, &level);
    level = next;
}
const out = level.items[0];
level.deinit(allocator);
return out;
```
   and add right after it:
```zig
/// Concatenates `refs` two at a time; an odd trailing ref is retained as-is.
fn mergePairs(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, refs: []const ArrayRef) Error!std.ArrayList(ArrayRef) {
    var out = try std.ArrayList(ArrayRef).initCapacity(allocator, (refs.len + 1) / 2);
    errdefer releaseAll(allocator, &out);
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i + 1 < refs.len) : (i += 2) {
        out.appendAssumeCapacity(try concatTwoArrayRefs(allocator, refs[i], refs[i + 1]));
    }
    if (i < refs.len) out.appendAssumeCapacity(refs[i].retain());
    return out;
}

fn releaseAll(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, list: *std.ArrayList(ArrayRef)) void {
    for (list.items) |*ref| ref.release();
    list.deinit(allocator);
}
```
2. In `nullCountForArray`, replace the per-bit `while` loop inside `if (data.validity()) |v| { ... }` with:
```zig
const end = data.offset + data.length;
if (bitmap.byteLength(end) > v.data.len) return error.InvalidInput;
return bitmap.countUnsetBitsInRange(v.data, data.offset, end);
```
3. Add the test `"concatArrayRefs merges many sliced parts in order"` (7 odd-count sliced Int32 parts with nulls) from perf.patch at the end of the file.
- Verify: `zig build test` and `$Z16 build test`: all pass, and the leak-checking `std.testing.allocator` reports no leaks.
- Done when: the new test passes on both compilers.

### T6: zero-copy IPC stream read (`src/ipc/stream_reader.zig`, benchmark)
- **Why:** the copying reader is memcpy-bound (~4.7 GB/s). For in-memory or mmap input the body bytes already exist, so slice them.
- **Measured:** read went from ~36-47 ms to ~3 ms (≈13x) in the default mode.
1. Add the field to `StreamReader` after `dictionary_values`:
   ```zig
   /// Set by `initZeroCopy`: `reader` is `std.Io.Reader.fixed(source.data)`, so message
